{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:77132d5ea8822b682aeefb00440436f1506b622fa3a5bb8fe882bea3ee3bca03","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.25},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.45},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.8}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"THE SINGLE STRONGEST SHARED ASSUMPTION ACROSS ALL THREE WAVE-1 PANELISTS, AND MY ATTACK ON IT. Strip the three answers to skeletons: P1 = r* 1.00 + TP 0.90 - wedge 0.50 = 1.40, blended to 1.95. P2 = r* 0.95 + TP 1.35 - wedge 0.35 = 1.95. P3 = r* 0.75 + TP 1.10 - wedge 0.20 = 1.65, +10bp artifact = 1.75. Three panelists, three lenses explicitly forbidden from using each other's methods, and all three put r* in a 0.75-1.00% band and all three declared the TERM PREMIUM the dominant variance source (55% / 45% / \"most\"). That unanimity is not convergent validity. It is one borrowed prior — the HLW/Laubach-Williams filtered r* plus the Rachel-Summers secular-stagnation accounting — that NONE of them interrogated. They then reinforced it with a shared rhetorical move: \"the brief's 2.60% anchor is a trap, the real G7-weighted level is 1.76-2.00%\", which all three treated as their headline finding and which all three resolved in the SAME downward direction on a genuinely ambiguous weighting basis. Three independent panelists resolving one ambiguity identically downward is anchoring on each other's framing dressed up as independent recomposition.\nWHY THE LOW-FLAT r* PRIOR IS THE WEAK JOINT. (i) It is a filter output, not an observation. HLW-class estimates are two-sided Kalman filters on realized data with standard errors around +/-1pp — wider than the entire quantity being forecast — and structurally incapable of detecting a regime break until years after it happens; the authors suspended publication in 2020 when the filter broke. The same filter has already been revising UP (roughly 0.4% in 2019 toward 1.3-1.4% by 2024-25). A lagging estimator currently revising upward is the worst possible anchor for a 2032-2036 average, and all three used its CURRENT read as their central value. (ii) The sample that produced it is the 1990-2019 global-savings-glut window: Chinese and EM reserve accumulation, a disinflating China, central banks absorbing duration. Every one of those reverses inside the locked 2A block. (iii) THE CLEAN CATEGORY ERROR. P2 explicitly cancels +35bp of life-cycle decumulation FLOW against -20bp of longer-retirement-horizon DESIRED-WEALTH effect (the Auclert-Malmberg-Martenet-Rognlie direction), and P1 and P3 do the same qualitatively. But the desired-wealth channel is a steady-state stock effect that was PRICED IN during the 1990-2020 accumulation phase — it is why rates fell then. The drawdown is the part still ahead. Netting a realized stock effect against an unrealized flow effect and reporting \"r* roughly flat\" double-counts the past. (iv) THE BOOKING ERROR, the structural heart of it. All three insist 2A's \"aging worsens global (r-g) by ~+1.0pp even where a frictionless r* eases\" licenses putting fiscal arithmetic exclusively in the premium. Read it again: even where a FRICTIONLESS r* eases. A frictionless r* clears desired saving against private investment in a model with no government paper. The equilibrium rate in an economy where the sovereign issues claims at 6-7% of GDP a year, with global gross debt past 100% of GDP and net interest heading to 3%, is the rate that clears saving against private investment PLUS government absorption — a LEVEL shift in r*, not compensation for uncertainty. Booking +25pp of G7 debt/GDP at a Laubach 2.5-4bp elasticity into \"term premium\" is an accounting convention the panel mistook for a finding about the world. P1 then used that same convention to justify a near-zero debt elasticity by citing Japan 1990-2020 (debt 65%->250%, real yields FELL) — but Japan's counterexample runs through a captive domestic buyer base, a current-account surplus and a deflating price level, i.e. precisely the three conditions 2A removes.\nMY DECOMPOSITION (G7 GDP-weighted, 2032-2036 average).\n(1) r* = 1.55%. US r* 1.75% (filter revising up; debt absorption booked where it belongs; boomer decumulation as an uncancelled flow). Euro area rises hardest in relative terms: Germany's post-2025 fiscal regime change, a deteriorating French path, Eurosystem QE runoff, taking the bloc from ~0.3 toward ~1.0. Japan from roughly -0.5 to +0.5 as the BoJ completes normalization. China's saving surplus is the single most likely piece to vanish: 2A locks pension subsidies at 10.1% of fiscal expenditure heading past 15%, and the glut was substantially a China/EM-reserve story. Downward channels I do price: precautionary saving under labour-income uncertainty (-15bp), cognitive-compensation compression raising the saving rate via the Mian-Straub-Sufi MPS gap (-9bp), post-bust excess capacity collapsing investment demand (-20bp in the digestion window).\n(2) Real term premium = 1.05% — BELOW P2's 1.35 and near P3's 1.10, because once fiscal supply moves into r* the premium is only compensation for real-rate uncertainty: elevated by rollover volume, eroding convenience yields, the levered basis trade as a fragile balance-sheet-contingent marginal buyer, and reduced central-bank absorption, plus P3's genuinely underrated aging channel on the asset side (closed DB schemes reaching funding and buying out, withdrawing a price-insensitive long-duration bid, +10-25bp). Capped because premium estimates rarely sustain above 1.5pp and because 2032-36 sits after the capex boom.\n(3) Repression wedge = -0.30%, and here I red-team the panel in the OTHER direction. P1 priced -0.55 expected (45% x 100bp + 30% x 25bp) and P2 -0.35 on a 0.60 probability. Two objections. First, repression operates on NOMINAL yields and CQ4 subtracts a breakeven: Japan under YCC 2016-2024 held 10y nominals at 0-0.25% and still frequently printed a POSITIVE measured real yield, because the yen BEI sat near zero. Repression suppresses the measured REAL yield only when paired with an inflation overshoot the central bank tolerates — the CQ2-conjunct-A world my own answer puts at 0.50, not 1.0. Second, binding US YCC on the 10y by 2032-36 requires the Fed to spend the inflation credibility that is the asset it is defending; P1's own base-rate evidence is 1945-1980 (pre-independence, pre-targeting) plus Japan plus euro-area SPREAD management, which is a different instrument on a different part of the curve. P(materially binding, G7-weighted) ~0.35 x conditional ~85bp = -30bp expected, with a fat left tail rather than a central case.\nCENTRAL: 1.55 + 1.05 - 0.30 = 2.30%, plus ~+15bp of measurement artifact (TIPS breakeven vs inflation swap, Italy's BTP-Bund credit spread entering the aggregate as though it were a real rate, Japan's illiquid BEI biasing its measured real yield high) and the weighting basis resolved toward MARKET-FX GDP weights rather than PPP. On weights: the lock fixes the real ACCOUNTING basis, but a sovereign yield is a local nominal market price and inherits nothing from a PPP conversion; the standard construction for a G7 yield aggregate is market-FX nominal GDP, lifting the US to ~0.60-0.64 and the level by ~25-30bp. All three Wave-1 panelists flagged this ambiguity and all three resolved it downward. P50 = 2.45%.\nPATH CHECK, WHERE P2 CONTRADICTS HIMSELF. P2 writes that \"most of the 2032-36 change is Japanese and euro-area convergence UP, not US mean reversion\" — then reports 1.95%, i.e. no change at all. Price his own sentence: Japan 0.5->1.8 (x0.11 = +14bp), Germany 0.8->1.6 (+9bp), France +10bp, Italy +6bp, UK +4bp, Canada +3bp is roughly +45-50bp on the weighted average with the US unchanged. He neutralized it with an assumed US mean reversion to 1.9-2.1 whose only mechanism is \"the 2026 term premium normalizes\" — while the entire 2A block says the premium's drivers INTENSIFY through 2031 (US debt 142%, net interest to 3% of GDP, convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade fragility, P95 Debt-at-Risk 117% rising 2.4pp on a 20% AI-led equity shock). You cannot hold both.\nVARIANCE ATTRIBUTION — MY DISTINGUISHING CLAIM: r* ~45%, real term premium ~30%, repression wedge ~25%. The unanimous Wave-1 verdict was premium-dominant. I say the r* LEVEL carries the most variance precisely because it is unobservable, estimated by a lagging filter with standard errors wider than the forecast quantity, and because the debt-absorption channel belongs there. I still respect 2A's distinction — 2A's +1.0pp (r-g) claim is fiscal arithmetic and premium, not an r* mechanism — and I honour it by NOT inflating the premium: I let the equilibrium rate itself reprice, which is the stronger version of the same claim.\nBANDS. P10 = 1.25: the filter was right all along, repression binds in the US and euro area simultaneously after an AI-led equity shock, post-bust precautionary saving swamps the decumulation flow. P90 = 3.80: no repression, convenience yield erodes hard, Japan and Europe converge fully, US real 10y above 4%. Five-year arithmetic averaging kills the spike tails and is why P90 stops at 3.80.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.554Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"The New York Fed's HLW r* estimate for the United States (or the Richmond Fed's Lubik-Matthes companion) is published at or below 1.0% for the 2027 and 2028 vintages, with no upward revision to the 2024-26 history.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The cleanest direct refutation of my central claim that the filter is revising upward into a regime break. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.45% toward 1.95-2.05% (Wave-1 territory), my variance attribution flips back to premium-dominant, CQ5 falls ~5pp to roughly 25 as the MOVE level channel weakens, and CQ6-D rises from 10 toward 15 at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) averages below 1.70% over any rolling twelve months, in the ABSENCE of an announced yield-curve-control, duration-shortening or captive-buyer mandate from the Federal Reserve or Treasury.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Mean reversion down without a repression mechanism is exactly the move Wave 1 asserted and I said had no mechanism. CQ4 P50 down ~50bp to ~1.95% and P90 down to ~3.2; CQ6-D up ~6pp and CQ6-A down ~4pp, since cheap duration signals abundant saving. If it instead happens WITH an announced mandate, my repression wedge was too small (-30bp) and only CQ4 moves, not CQ3 or CQ6-A.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The OECD's published GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation aggregate for 2028-2030 prints below 2.2%/yr on the Turkiye-INCLUSIVE basis, or the OECD discontinues the Turkiye-inclusive headline aggregate in favour of the ex-Turkiye series as its reference measure.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Either observation kills the measurement argument that took my CQ2 to 34 (top of the Wave-1 range) and that suppressed CQ6-D. CQ2 falls to ~24, CQ6-C falls from 19 to ~14 and CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~15. This is the signpost most specific to me, because two of three Wave-1 panelists never priced Turkiye at all.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >=25% occurs and G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yields fall by >=100bp from the equity peak date to the trough date — i.e. the bond hedge works emphatically, not marginally.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms Wave 1's disinflationary-bust premise against my credit-event argument and resolves CQ5's conjunct B as NO for the likeliest shock in the window. CQ5 falls from 30 to ~18. It also implies abundant precautionary saving, so CQ4 P50 falls ~35bp and CQ6-D rises from 10 toward 16.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Japan's 10y JGB nominal yield remains below 2.25% AND Germany's 10y Bund below 3.00% on a twelve-month average, with the Bank of Japan holding a policy rate at or below 1.0%.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the Japan/Europe convergence-upward component worth ~40-50bp of my CQ4 recomposition and vindicates the low-weighted-average reading all three Wave-1 panelists reached (by a route panelist 2 described in words and then neutralized). CQ4 P50 down ~40bp to ~2.05%; CQ5 down ~4pp via the MOVE level channel; CQ3 and CQ6 essentially unchanged, since this is a price signpost, not a quantity one.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 redteam seat, effort 'rstar'."}}